{"name":"Wingsuit Wind","hex":"#bad5d4","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wingsuit","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A special jumpsuit, used in extreme sports like a parachute, which adds surface area to the wearer's body to create lift."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wingsuit"},{"term":"wind","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Real or perceived movement of atmospheric air usually caused by convection or differences in air pressure.","Air artificially put in motion by any force or action."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To blow air through a wind instrument or horn to make a sound.","To cause (someone) to become breathless, as by a blow to the abdomen, or by physical exertion, running, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wind"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wingsuit flying","blocks":[{"text":"Wingsuit flying, also called wingsuiting, is flight in free fall using a specialized suit with fabric wings between the arms and torso and between the legs. The added surface area reduces vertical speed and increases horizontal speed, producing a controllable glide while restricting part of the jumper's normal range of motion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Wingsuit_Flying_over_Langar_Airfield_UK.jpg/330px-Wingsuit_Flying_over_Langar_Airfield_UK.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wingsuit_flying","wikidata":"Q592031"},{"title":"Wind","blocks":[{"text":"Wind is the natural movement of air or other gases relative to a planet's surface. Winds occur on a range of scales, from thunderstorm flows lasting tens of minutes, to local breezes generated by heating of land surfaces and lasting a few hours, to global winds resulting from the difference in absorption of solar energy between the climate zones on Earth. The study of wind is known as anemology."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif/330px-Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind","wikidata":"Q8094"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Franz_Reichelt_%28high_res%29.jpg/500px-Franz_Reichelt_%28high_res%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Franz_Reichelt_%28high_res%29.jpg/1280px-Franz_Reichelt_%28high_res%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Franz_Reichelt_%28high_res%29.jpg/500px-Franz_Reichelt_%28high_res%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Franz Reichelt before his 1912 jump from the Eiffel Tower","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFranz_Reichelt_(high_res).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Wingsuit-01.jpg/330px-Wingsuit-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Wingsuit-01.jpg/330px-Wingsuit-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wingsuit pilots in flight after an aircraft exit","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWingsuit-01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Steph_Davis_wingsuit_BASE_brento.jpg/500px-Steph_Davis_wingsuit_BASE_brento.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Steph_Davis_wingsuit_BASE_brento.jpg/500px-Steph_Davis_wingsuit_BASE_brento.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wingsuit BASE jumping from a cliff","link":"https://en.wikip